\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Neutral SU(N) loop correlations}
\date{2026-11-11}

\p{Take #{SU(N)}, #{N\ge2}, on a periodic cubic spatial
 lattice #{(\mathbb Z/L)^3}, #{L\ge3}, with positively
 oriented coordinate links and reverse transport given by
 inversion. Let #{\mathcal P_s} contain one orientation and
 basepoint for each elementary spatial plaquette. For its
 [ordered holonomy](fdip-006F) #{U_p}, put
##{F(U)=\sum_{p\in\mathcal P_s}
 \left(1-\frac1N\operatorname{Re}\chi_{\mathrm{fund}}(U_p)\right),
 \qquad\chi_{\mathrm{fund}}=\trace.}
Endpoint cancellation makes each closed holonomy transform by
 conjugation, so #{F} is gauge invariant. Unitarity gives
 #{|\trace(U_p)|\le N}, and therefore
 #{0\le F\le2|\mathcal P_s|}. It defines a bounded
 self-adjoint multiplication operator on the physical space.}

\p{Choose the [heat-kernel transfer](fdip-00ML) and a bounded
 real Borel spatial potential invariant under lattice
 translations, signed permutations of the three coordinate
 directions and charge conjugation. Signed coordinate
 permutations act on links as in \ref{fdip-00NC}; parity
 is #{x\mapsto-x}. The isotropic Wilson potential
 #{V=\beta F}, #{\beta\ge0}, is one such choice.
 These transformations permute plaquettes up to basepoint
 change and orientation reversal. Basepoint change conjugates
 holonomy, while reversal replaces it by its inverse. Moreover
##{\chi_{\mathrm{fund}}(\overline{U_p})
 =\overline{\chi_{\mathrm{fund}}(U_p)}
 =\chi_{\mathrm{fund}}(U_p^{-1}).}
The real trace is therefore invariant under all these actions.
 The potential symmetry and \ref{fdip-00NC} show that the
 transfer commutes with their unitaries and the vacuum is fixed.}

\p{With #{F_c=F-\int F\Omega^2\,d\nu}, the centered vector
 #{F_c\Omega} lies in the translation-invariant cubic scalar,
 parity-even and charge-conjugation-even subspace. In the
 notation of \ref{fdip-00N7}, its correlation is
##{\langle\Omega,M_{F_c}S^nM_{F_c}\Omega\rangle
 =\sum_{0<r<1}r^n\|E_r(F_c\Omega)\|^2.}
Only spectral components in that common fixed subspace occur.
 The periodic link insertion formula and its vacuum limit are
 those of \ref{fdip-0083} and \ref{fdip-00N7}. A different
 real gauge-invariant potential need not have the stated
 spatial or charge-conjugation symmetries.}

\p{For #{SU(2)}, the antiunitary map on its fundamental space
 #{\mathbb C^2}
##{J(z_1,z_2)=(-\overline z_2,\overline z_1)}
satisfies #{J^2=-\bbI} and commutes with every group element,
 as direct substitution in the standard #{SU(2)} form shows.
 It induces the complex-linear equivariant map
 #{\overline{\mathbb C^2}\to\mathbb C^2},
 #{\overline v\mapsto Jv}. Thus this representation is
 quaternionic self-conjugate and its character equals its
 complex conjugate. For #{N\ge3}, the center element
 #{z\bbI}, #{z=\ue^{2\pi\ui /N}}, acts by #{z} on the fundamental
 and by #{\overline z\ne z} on its conjugate.
 An intertwiner would satisfy #{zA=\overline zA}, forcing
 #{A=0}. The fundamental is therefore not self-conjugate;
 the real-part prescription above still gives a C-even
 observable for every #{N}.}

\p{The cyclic center also leads to a separate finite-group
 comparison: the [cyclic lattice duality](fdip-00NO) exchanges
 link characters with dual flux sectors. Its relation to
 electric and magnetic variables is qualified in
 \ref{fdip-00NR}.}

\p{An invariant sum over any finite symmetry orbit of closed
 spatial loops gives the same type of bounded neutral observable.
 If the graph is a tree, \ref{fdip-00N8} instead gives only
 the zero centered correlation. The physical meaning of the
 cubic channel is discussed in \ref{fdip-00NG}.}
